---
<Base title={title} description={description} jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <article class="wrap article power-guide">
    <span class="eyebrow">ROG Ally X power and flight guide</span>
    <h1>ROG Ally X power bank requirements: 65W output, watt-hours, and airline rules</h1>
    <p class="lede muted">A useful Ally X power bank must pass two different checks: enough USB-C Power Delivery output to support the handheld and a clearly labeled energy capacity that fits the trip. This is official-spec and policy-informed guidance, not a hands-on battery test.</p>
    <p class="affiliate-note">Disclosure: shopping links are broad Amazon searches marked sponsored/nofollow. They are not endorsements, live prices, or claims that every result is compatible. Handheld Settings Lab appends an Associates tag only when a real tag is configured.</p>

    <div class="callout"><strong>Fast answer:</strong> start with a bank that supplies at least <strong>65W from one USB-C PD port</strong>, offers a compatible 20V profile, shows its watt-hour rating on the body, and stays at or below <strong>100Wh</strong> for the simplest U.S. airline case. Pair it with a suitably rated USB-C cable and test the exact setup before travel.</div>

    <h2>The four checks that matter</h2>
    <div class="requirements-grid">
      <section class="requirement"><span>01</span><h3>Single-port output</h3><p>At least 65W from the port connected to the Ally X—not a combined total across every connector.</p></section>
      <section class="requirement"><span>02</span><h3>USB-C PD profile</h3><p>ASUS lists 20V at 3.25A for its 65W adapter. Read the bank’s output table rather than relying on its product name.</p></section>
      <section class="requirement"><span>03</span><h3>Printed watt-hours</h3><p>Use the Wh value on the bank body for airline checks. mAh alone does not state stored energy without voltage.</p></section>
      <section class="requirement"><span>04</span><h3>Cable and port plan</h3><p>Use a properly rated cable, the bank’s correct high-output port, and no shared load during the first verification.</p></section>
    </div>

    <h2>65W is the compatibility baseline, not a runtime promise</h2>
    <p>ASUS’s official ROG Ally X specification lists an <strong>80Wh internal battery</strong> and a <strong>65W USB-C adapter with 20V DC at 3.25A</strong>. That makes 65W USB-C PD output a sensible external-power baseline. It does not mean the Ally X always draws 65W, that a larger bank charges it faster, or that a 65W bank guarantees positive charging during every workload.</p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Label on a bank</th><th>What it tells you</th><th>What to verify</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>65W USB-C PD</td><td>Potentially matches the official adapter’s power class</td><td>65W is available from one port and the output table includes a compatible 20V profile</td></tr>
        <tr><td>100W USB-C PD</td><td>May provide useful headroom for a dock or another device</td><td>The Ally X port still gets at least 65W under the intended port-sharing arrangement</td></tr>
        <tr><td>20,000mAh</td><td>Charge quantity at an unstated or separately stated voltage</td><td>The actual printed Wh value; mAh alone is not the airline limit</td></tr>
        <tr><td>99Wh</td><td>Nominal stored energy listed by the maker</td><td>Legible label, current airline policy, physical condition, and carry-on placement</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Watt-hours versus watts: do not mix the two</h2>
    <p><strong>Watts (W)</strong> describe how quickly power can be supplied. <strong>Watt-hours (Wh)</strong> describe nominal stored energy. A bank can have plenty of capacity but an output too weak for gaming, or excellent 100W output but too little capacity for the trip.</p>
    <div class="formula-card">
      <strong>Watt-hour arithmetic</strong>
      <p><code>Wh = (mAh ÷ 1,000) × nominal battery voltage</code></p>
      <p>Example only: 20,000mAh is 20Ah. At a stated nominal 3.7V, <code>20Ah × 3.7V = 74Wh</code>. Do not substitute this example for the manufacturer’s printed Wh rating because cell configuration and labeling differ.</p>
    </div>
    <p>Usable output energy is lower than the nameplate capacity because voltage conversion, cable loss, battery management, temperature, and operating overhead consume energy. Do not advertise or plan around “one full extra charge” from equal-looking Wh figures.</p>

    <h2>ROG Ally X power bank airline checklist</h2>
    <p>The following summarizes current U.S. FAA and TSA guidance fetched on August 29, 2026. It does not replace a carrier’s rules or the laws at a connection or destination.</p>
    <ol class="flight-list">
      <li><strong>Put the power bank in carry-on baggage.</strong> TSA says “Carry On Bags: Yes” and “Checked Bags: No.” FAA says spare batteries and power banks must be carried in the cabin.</li>
      <li><strong>Remove it if your carry-on is gate-checked.</strong> FAA says power banks must be removed from a bag checked at the gate or planeside and kept with the passenger in the cabin.</li>
      <li><strong>Use the printed Wh rating.</strong> FAA limits ordinary rechargeable lithium-ion batteries to 100Wh per battery.</li>
      <li><strong>Ask before packing a larger bank.</strong> FAA says batteries over 100Wh through 160Wh require airline approval, with a limit of two spare larger lithium-ion batteries per person.</li>
      <li><strong>Protect against short circuits and damage.</strong> Cover or isolate terminals, keep loose metal away, and do not travel with a swollen, punctured, recalled, leaking, or overheating bank.</li>
      <li><strong>Check every governing rule.</strong> Confirm the airline, country, and connection policies shortly before departure; U.S. agency guidance is not a universal international policy.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>How to test the power bank before relying on it</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Charge both devices normally.</strong> Begin with a stable Ally X, current system updates, and no large background download.</li>
      <li><strong>Use the bank’s documented high-output USB-C port.</strong> Some ports are input-only or have different limits.</li>
      <li><strong>Connect directly.</strong> Remove the dock, hub, phone, and second cable so port sharing cannot hide the first result.</li>
      <li><strong>Use one known-good rated cable.</strong> An unmarked phone cable can become the bottleneck even when both endpoints support enough power.</li>
      <li><strong>Check Windows and Armoury Crate status.</strong> Confirm charging is detected and note any warning. Then run a representative game area—not only the menu—for several minutes.</li>
      <li><strong>Repeat under the mode you travel with.</strong> A desktop idle check does not prove the bank can keep up with an active game and bright display.</li>
      <li><strong>Feel for abnormal heat and cable strain.</strong> Warmth can be normal; rapid overheating, odor, swelling, intermittent connectors, or a damaged cable is a stop condition.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>If the Ally X drains while plugged into the bank</h2>
    <div class="troubleshoot">
      <section><h3>Wrong output port</h3><p>Move to the bank’s specifically labeled high-output USB-C port. Do not assume identical-looking ports share the same specification.</p></section>
      <section><h3>Shared output</h3><p>Disconnect the phone or second device. Multi-port banks commonly redistribute their total output when another port becomes active.</p></section>
      <section><h3>Cable bottleneck</h3><p>Replace only the cable with a known-good suitably rated USB-C cable. Keep the bank and Ally X unchanged so the test isolates one variable.</p></section>
      <section><h3>Dock overhead</h3><p>Test bank-to-Ally directly. A dock and its display, Ethernet, storage, or USB devices consume part of the incoming power budget.</p></section>
      <section><h3>Thermal or low-charge derating</h3><p>Let the bank cool, move it out of an insulating bag, and retest at a moderate state of charge. Some banks reduce output near empty or when hot.</p></section>
      <section><h3>Workload exceeds incoming power</h3><p>Use a stable frame cap and sensible power mode. A slowly falling battery can indicate that the active system load is greater than negotiated input.</p></section>
    </div>
    <p>Follow the <a href="/guides/rog-ally-armoury-crate-power-profiles/">ROG Ally power-profile guide</a> before changing random Windows options. For cable details, use the <a href="/guides/usb-c-cable-wattage-handheld-gaming/">USB-C cable wattage checklist</a>.</p>

    <h2>Estimate added runtime without inventing a benchmark</h2>
    <p>Do not divide the bank’s Wh by a processor TDP and call the result battery life. The display, memory, storage, fan, radios, speakers, conversion losses, and background work all use power. Instead, observe <strong>total system draw</strong> in a repeatable game area and enter a conservative usable-energy assumption.</p>
    <div class="callout"><strong>Planning tool:</strong> use the <a href="/calculators/battery-estimator/">battery-life estimator</a> with observed total draw and a safety margin. Then read the <a href="/guides/rog-ally-battery-life-calculator-guide/">ROG Ally 40Wh versus Ally X 80Wh calculator guide</a>. Neither page claims a personally measured runtime.</div>

    <h2>Spec-based shopping starting points</h2>
    <p>These broad searches reflect the compatibility checks above. Verify the exact model, revision, per-port output table, printed Wh label, cable requirements, warranty, seller, and return policy. No current price, availability, or hands-on result is asserted.</p>
    <div class="shopping-grid">
      <a class="shopping-card" href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=65w+usb+c+pd+power+bank+rog+ally+x+100wh" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored" data-affiliate-link data-category="power-banks"><span>Sponsored search</span><h3>65W USB-C PD Ally X power banks</h3><p>Start with single-port output and printed Wh, not accessory bundles.</p></a>
      <a class="shopping-card" href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=100w+usb+c+pd+power+bank+under+100wh" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored" data-affiliate-link data-category="power-banks"><span>Sponsored search</span><h3>100W PD banks under 100Wh</h3><p>Useful only when the intended port allocation still supports the Ally X.</p></a>
      <a class="shopping-card" href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=100w+usb+c+cable+e-marker" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored" data-affiliate-link data-category="usb-c-cables-adapters"><span>Sponsored search</span><h3>100W-rated USB-C cables</h3><p>Compare length, e-marker disclosure, connector strain relief, and warranty.</p></a>
    </div>
    <p>For a central compatibility shortlist, visit the <a href="/gear/power-banks/">power-bank gear guide</a>. A broad search result is not proof that a product supplies the advertised wattage on every port or complies with every airline.</p>

    <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
    <div class="grid cols2">
      {faqs.map(faq => <section class="card"><h3>{faq.question}</h3><p>{faq.answer}</p></section>)}
    </div>

    <h2>Sources and method</h2>
    <p>ASUS, FAA, and TSA public pages were fetched successfully on August 29, 2026. ASUS supplied the official Ally X 80Wh battery, 65W adapter, 20V/3.25A output, and USB-C Power Delivery details. FAA and TSA supplied the U.S. carry-on, 100Wh, larger-battery approval, gate-check, and terminal-protection rules. USB-IF is included for general USB Power Delivery context. The purchase and test order is conservative editorial guidance; no power bank was personally tested, and no search volume or fixed runtime is claimed.</p>
    <ul>
      {sources.map(source => <li><a href={source.url} target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener">{source.label}</a></li>)}
    </ul>

    <div class="callout"><strong>Next step:</strong> stabilize charging, updates, and the chosen power mode first, then use the <a href="/rog-ally-settings/">ROG Ally settings hub</a> to tune the game rather than using a larger battery to mask an unstable setup.</div>
  </article>
</Base>

